Writer: a .docx whose runs carry many distinct colours no longer takes minutes to open.

Every run with a colour stores the original value in its character grab bag (CharThemeOriginalColor),
and the import puts each run's attributes into the item sharing (HashedItemInstanceManager) and into
the autostyle pool (StylePool). Both searched linearly among the distinct items:
- SfxGrabBagItem::hashCode() hashed only the keys, so all these grab bags shared one hash bucket and
  were compared one by one (Any comparison, uno_type_equalData);
- StylePool::Node::findChildNode() compared a new item with every child of the node, one child per
  distinct colour.
The import was therefore O(N^2) in distinct run colours, which OCR output and text pasted from web
pages or PDF files have in thousands. With the 1.0.2 batch5 build on Linux (4 CPUs) a synthetic
0.4 MB document of 2500 paragraphs (20 000 runs, a colour each) took 244 s to load, frozen the whole
time; 1000 paragraphs took 14.5 s, but 3.9 s without the colours.

- SfxGrabBagItem::hashCode() also hashes string values (equal Anys of other types may differ in their
  type, so only strings are added; equal items keep equal hash codes).
- StylePool nodes keep their children whose item supports hashCode() in an unordered_multimap keyed by
  hash and Which; other items keep the linear search. Child order (mChildren, used by the iterators)
  is unchanged.

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0); the new test file is MPL-2.0.
Test: CppunitTest_svl_items (test_grabbagitem: equal bags hash equally, different string values
hash differently); the sw import/export suites; tests/unit/test_large_office_open_patches.py.
---
--- a/svl/source/items/grabbagitem.cxx
+++ b/svl/source/items/grabbagitem.cxx
@@ -45,7 +45,16 @@
 {
     std::size_t seed(0);
     for (const auto& rPair : m_aMap)
+    {
         o3tl::hash_combine(seed, rPair.first.hashCode());
+        // HST Office: string values too. With the keys alone every run of a .docx that keeps its
+        // original colour in the grab bag (CharThemeOriginalColor) had the same hash, and the
+        // item sharing and the autostyle pool compared them one by one: O(N^2) in distinct
+        // colours. Only strings: equal Anys of other types may differ in type (equal hashes).
+        OUString aValue;
+        if (rPair.second.getValueTypeClass() == uno::TypeClass_STRING && (rPair.second >>= aValue))
+            o3tl::hash_combine(seed, aValue.hashCode());
+    }
     return seed;
 }
 
